   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\nestablishing that an applicant for a license or permit shall not be deemed to\nlack control of the premises under certain circumstances",
    "basePrintNo": "S6353",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6353-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §110, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6353\n\nSPONSOR: PERSAUD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\nestablishing that an applicant for a license or permit shall not be\ndeemed to lack control of the premises under certain circumstances\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nEstablishes that an applicant for a license or permit shall not be\ndeemed to lack control of the premises under certain circumstances.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends Paragraph (g) of subdivision 1 of section 110 of the\nalcoholic beverage control law, as amended by chapter 196 of the laws of\n2011 and as relettered by chapter 188 of the laws of 2013, to deem an\napplicant in control of a portion of a divided public sidewalk or road-\nway when the respective municipality has given the applicant exclusive\ncontrol.\n\nSection 2 provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThousands of restaurants in New York City - and many more throughout the\nstate - have depended on outdoor dining to remain economically viable as\nCOVID-19 related restrictions continue to force unprecedented hardship\non the industry. In New York City, approximately 11,000 restaurants\nparticipate in the City's \"Open Restaurants\" outdoor dining program.\nThis program has been responsible for saving countless restaurants and\nbringing back over 100,000 industry jobs.\n\nThe existing Executive Order currently allows for establishments that\nare licensed to sell liquor for on premises consumption to serve alcohol\nin non-contiguous areas such as a restaurant's roadway seating. Addi-\ntionally, and equally as important, it allows for restaurants to serve\nalcohol on sidewalks contiguous to the restaurant without having to file\nfor an alteration application with the State Liquor Authority. The abil-\nity for these restaurants to serve alcohol in non-contiguous spaces and\nauthorized contiguous public spaces is absolutely crucial to the contin-\nued survival of New York City's restaurant industry.\n\nWithout a permanently codified authorization to serve alcohol in these\nareas, many restaurants will not have an economically viable business\nmodel leading to more restaurants closing, more jobs lost, the\ndestruction of the City's cultural fabric, and an exponential increase\nin the number of vacant storefronts.\n\nGiven current resource levels, it would be difficult to imagine the SLA\nprocessing 11,000 concurrent applications in a timely manner, while\nrestaurants cease serving alcohol pending approval. Without the ability\nto serve alcohol in spaces that are non-contiguous, many if not most of\nthe small businesses in the restaurant sector that have utilized outdoor\ndining will be forced to terminate operations.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNone.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
