    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6353A\n\nSPONSOR: PERSAUD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act in relation to permitting the use of municipal space for outdoor\ndining; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon the expira-\ntion thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe bill would enable those businesses licensed by the State Liquor\nAuthority to sell alcohol for on-site consumption to use municipal space\nfor outdoor dining.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 contains the legislative intent of this bill.\n\nSection 2 provides that businesses licensed to sell alcoholic beverages\nfor on-premises consumption may apply to the State Liquor Authority\n(\"the Authority\") to operate on municipal property for the purposes of\noutdoor dining. Licensees must have permits from the municipality for\nthe use of such space.\n\nSuch outdoor space is limited to space contiguous to the licensee's\npremises as already permitted by § 99d of the Alcoholic Beverage Control\nLaw or space that is 1) within close proximity to, and in line of sight\nof, the licensee's premises, 2) extend to the sides no more than the\nwidth of the licensed premises and no further than the center line of\nthe roadway, and 3) provide thoroughfare for pedestrians, customers, and\nemployees.\n\nApplicants would need to prove that the outdoor space meets all federal,\nstate, and local laws, rules, and guidance, and provide a current tempo-\nrary use permit from the municipality.\n\nNew applicants would also need to provide community notification in a\nmanner consistent with § 110-b of the Alcoholic Beverage Control Law.\nThose licensees already using outdoor space under authority granted by\nExecutive Order 202.38 would not need to reapply and may continue to use\noutdoor space already approved, unless 1) the municipality revokes its\npermit, 2) the applicant seeks to change the municipal permit, or 3) the\nlicensee renews its municipal permit.  The Authority retains its ability\nto seek to revoke or suspend a licensee's ability to use outdoor space\nin accordance with § 119 of the alcoholic beverage control law.\n\nSection 3 contains the effective date of the bill.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nRestaurants have been hit particularly hard by the pandemic. Many have\nclosed, but many more are limping along. The restrictions on capacity\nand consumer reluctance have meant that few people want to dine indoors.\n\nHowever, an important lifeline for those restaurants that have made it\nthrough the pandemic has been the ability to operate' outdoors on side-\nwalks and streets. Many municipalities have been willing to assist\nrestaurants in this regard, allowing the use of municipal outdoor space.\nHowever, those restaurants with to sell alcohol are restricted from\noperating outdoors in streets or sidewalks. The Governor allowed these\nrestaurants to operate outdoors through executive order, which was\nextremely helpful. But, that privilege will last only as long as the\nGovernor's Executive Order is in effect.  This bill will make sure these\nrestaurants, who employ so many New Yorkers, will be able to keep this\nprivilege for another year and find a way back to stability and recov-\nery.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall expire and be deemed\nrepealed one year after it shall have become a law.\n",
